# jrnl — real-world examples

## Add an entry via your configured editor

```sh
jrnl
```

## Add an entry straight from the command line

```sh
jrnl Write entry here.
jrnl today: Rebuilt the mediaserver RAID array. @homelab
```

## Tags

```sh
jrnl --tags       # list all tags
jrnl @homelab     # entries with a tag
```

## Export the journal

```sh
jrnl --export json
jrnl --export markdown
```

## Entries in a timeframe

```sh
jrnl -from 2025 -until may
jrnl -from "last monday"
```

## Configure the editor (~/.config/jrnl/jrnl.yaml, older: .jrnl_config)

```yaml
# Linux
editor: "vim"
# Windows example
editor: "F:\\Powerpack\\Sublime\\sublime_text.exe -w"
```

## Homelab: quick log of maintenance work

```sh
jrnl Patched homelab with zypper dup, kernel 6.x, reboot clean. @homelab @maintenance
```

| Flag / arg    | What it does               |
| ------------- | -------------------------- |
| @tag          | tag an entry / filter by tag |
| --tags        | list tags with counts      |
| -from / -until| time range filter          |
| --export FMT  | export (json, markdown, ...) |
| -n <n>        | show last n entries        |
